Work through, in this order:

1. What is the single strongest argument this material supports, and which claims
   carry it? Start there. Everything below is a test of whether that one article
   holds, not a search for how many articles could be extracted.
2. What evidence does each argument have? Name the supporting claims by their
   claim id. An argument whose claims are all interpretations or opinions is a
   thin article however interesting it sounds.
3. Where do the arguments overlap, and can each stand alone for a reader who has
   not read the other?
4. What must the reader already know? State it plainly; it decides how much of
   the article is preamble.
5. Are there competing theses — readings of the same material that would produce
   a different article? Name them even when you reject them.
6. What is the thin-content risk for each: low, medium or high?

Then ask whether one article is enough, and answer it against this bar.

Propose a second article only if a single one would have to **drop material the
reader needs to believe the thesis**. That is the test, and nothing else is:

- Not that the source contains other interesting topics. It will.
- Not that the material would overrun the target length. An article may leave
  material out and say so; the draft records what it omitted and why, and a
  focused article with declared omissions is better than two thin ones.
- Not that the topics are separable. Most are, and separating them is what
  strips an article of the concrete detail that made it worth reading — the
  evidence follows the topic, so the piece left arguing *about* a mechanism
  keeps none of the artefacts that show it working.

If you do propose more than one, the second must fail without the first — a
reader who has read article one must still need article two. Propose the fewest
that clears that bar, and say in your rationale what a single article would have
had to drop.

Then decide. Your decision must name:

- the article you recommend leading with, by id;
- the alternatives you considered and why you rejected each — an alternative
  with no reason is not a record of a decision;
- your confidence, between 0 and 1, as a number you would defend;
- what you are uncertain about, in the author's terms.

If you propose more than one article and they belong in sequence, say so and give
a reading order covering every one of them.

